# SYNOPSIS

```text
fs [--format LABEL [--encrypt]] [--check] [--daily HH:MM] PARTITION ...

status
ctl
volume/...
snap/YYYY/MMDD[.N]/...
send
receive/<sys>
```

# DESCRIPTION
devmgr binds disks a moment into the boot, so fs waits up to 10 s for a partition, and with several named, takes the first there.
The fs service serves a Taisce volume, the one file system, kept on a disk's partition in its namespace: the manifest's is the virtio disk's `/dev/sdF0/taisce` (virtio-blk(3)), which vx-disk(1) makes with a volume on it; in the board images (board-test(8)) it is the first of `/dev/sdN0/taisce` and `/dev/sdN1/taisce` there (nvme(3)), formatted the first time.
devmgr binds the disk a moment into the boot, so fs waits for the partition, up to 10 s, before it gives up.
With `--format`, a partition that holds no volume gets a new one labelled `LABEL`; otherwise one that holds none is an error.
With `--encrypt` too, the new volume is encrypted (taisce(4)), and fs makes it when the first `unlock` written to `ctl` gives its passphrase, which becomes its first slot.
An encrypted volume is mounted only once a passphrase one of its slots takes is typed at the console's prompt or written to `ctl` as `unlock`, or a swarm slot's member answers for it.
fs says `fs: <partition>: encrypted; its passphrase is asked for on the console`, and asks `passphrase for <partition>: ` through kbdfs(4)'s `cons`, which its manifest mounts at `/mnt/kbdfs`, with raw mode on, so what is typed is not shown; Backspace and `^U` edit the line, and a passphrase no slot takes is asked for again.
Meanwhile fs says `fs: <partition>: asking the swarm member at <address> too` for each swarm slot, and asks each slot's member through `/net`, which its manifest mounts if the image has the network stack (ip(4)).
A request is the member's key and a blinded point, 64 bytes, to the slot's address; the member's answer, a point, opens the slot (taisce(4)), and fs says `unlocked by the swarm member at <address>`.
Until one answers, fs asks again, the wait between rounds doubling from a second to half a minute, and says why each member was not asked once: `<address>: no answer; asking again`, or the dial's error.
The member's side is swarm(4)'s.
Until then the tree answers, `status` says `volume locked` (`locked, new` for one yet to be made), and walks into `volume`, `snap`, `send` and `receive` wait, as do the services that mount `/data`: the console's shell, cpu(8)'s server and the rest start once it is unlocked.
With `--check`, every invariant of the volume is checked once it is mounted, as fsck would (Taisce's trees, its catalog, and that the blocks in use are exactly those the trees hold), and fs says `fs: <partition>: check passed, <n> nodes, <ms> ms` or `check FAILED:` and what failed, on the console and in `status` (`check running` until then).
The tree answers at once, so the launcher hears fs however long the check takes; the volume's own thread runs the check before it answers a request, so its clients wait for it, and it is served either way.
The board images pass it, so each boot after a power cut checks the volume (crash-test(8)).
With `--daily`, a snapshot is taken each day at `HH:MM` UTC, by a thread asleep until then; the cpu and board images take one at 03:00.
It reads and writes the partition through the partition channel's ring (nodefile(2)), each read and write waited for; through batches of messages if the disk won't attach one.
Each barrier the volume needs (before and after a group's commit, and an intent log's write) is a `flush` written to the disk's `ctl`, beside the partition (sd(3)).
A file's channel takes a ring too, as a client moving its bulk attaches (`cp`, FDIO): each entry is read or written as a batch's would be, and answered at once.

Its tree holds:

: `status`
  Until the volume is mounted, `volume locked` and its device.
  Then the volume's label, its device, the last committed transaction group (`txg`), the channels and fids open on it (`sessions`), its live queries (`live-queries`), the files whose bytes it keeps in memory (`files`), the bytes of theirs in memory (`resident`, asked of the kernel at most once a second, as a file is opened or closed or a group commits) and those written but not yet committed (`dirty`), then `paged` when a pager fills them on demand, the groups committed since it started (`commits`), its blocks, free blocks and block size, and how many snapshots it holds (`snapshots`); on an encrypted volume, `keybag` and each of its four slots, `passphrase`, `swarm=`<address> or `empty`; then a line for each of the last four `ctl` requests and streams received, `snap` <name> `taken`, `unsnap` <name> `deleted`, `slot` <n> `passphrase added`, `slot` <n> `swarm` <address> `added`, `slot` <n> `removed`, `receive` <sys> `to txg` <txg> `done, snap` <name>, or what failed; with `--check`, the check's line.
: `ctl`
  Takes `snap`, which commits and keeps the volume as that commit left it, and `unsnap` <name>, which deletes the snapshot of that name (`2026/1030`) and frees what only it kept.
  On an encrypted volume it takes `slot add passphrase` <words>, which wraps the volume key in the first empty slot under the rest of the line, spaces and all; `slot add swarm` <address> <key>, which wraps it in the first empty slot for the swarm member whose unlock key is <key>, 64 lowercase hex digits (its `unlock` line in swarm(4)'s `status`), and which answers at <address>, a dial string of at most 56 bytes (`tcp!192.168.0.31!17021`); and `slot remove` <n>, which empties slot <n> unless it is the last one filled.
  Each commits only the superblock.
  On a plain one they fail with `unsupported`.
  Any other line fails with `bad arg in system call`.
  The volume's thread runs each, so `ctl` answers at once and `status` says how it went.
  Before an encrypted volume is mounted, `ctl` takes only `unlock` <words>, the rest of the line the passphrase: a passphrase no slot takes fails with `permission denied`, and a second `unlock` while the first mounts fails with `file already exists`.
: `snap`
  The snapshots, as Plan 9's dump shows them: a directory for each year, and in it one for each snapshot, named by the day it was taken (UTC), `MMDD`, with `.1`, `.2` for a second and later one that day.
  Each is the volume's root as the commit before it left it: walks, stats, listings and reads, nothing else; a change fails with `permission denied`.
  A snapshot is a record of a commit, not a copy: what it sees stays on the disk until it is deleted, and a block the volume lets go that a snapshot sees is kept for it (taisce(4)).
  Manifests mount it as they mount the volume: `mount /n/dump fs/snap`.
: `send`
  A snapshot's stream, for send(1): write `[`<from>`]` <snapshot>, snapshots' names, then read the stream, in order, from offset 0, until a read gives nothing.
  Each open sends one stream.
: `receive/`<sys>
  A stream written to it, in order, from offset 0, is applied to the volume's `recv/`<sys>, made if it isn't there, which then takes a snapshot; one stream an open.
  Any name walks; a listing gives the volume's `recv/` directories.
: `volume`
  The volume's root directory: walks go on into the volume.

Manifests mount the volume at `/data` for creating in, `mount /data fs/volume create` (manifest(6)), so that `cp`, `mkdir` and `>` make files there.
The console's shell, cpu(8)'s sessions and the launcher's test have it.

The volume speaks node(5): walks, stats, listings, reads, writes, creating files and directories, removing them, and wstat's rename within a directory, modification time and length.
A listing reads each entry's node only for the stat fields it asks for: one of names and kinds comes from the directory alone.
The volume's tree nodes stay in memory as read, up to a 64th of the RAM and never less than 8 MiB, those not read for longest going first; a node read from the disk is checked against its checksum first.
A file's bytes, once read, stay in memory in a VMO of the file's own, at their own offsets, and its reads copy from there.
It is a pager's VMO (pager(2)): a page is read from the disk when first needed, by a thread of fs's own on a second channel to the partition, with the 64 KiB around it, and twice as much each time a read goes on from where the last one ended, up to 512 KiB.
The kernel takes back clean pages when memory is short, charged to fs's account (account(2)); the 4096 files used last keep their VMOs, and so does any file with pages written and not yet committed.
A write goes into the file's VMO, behind: its size and times show at once, and its pages reach the disk at the next commit or the file's sync, together with that size and times, so that a crash shows the file as it was before the write or after it, never its new size without its bytes.
A write goes to the volume at once instead when the file has no VMO, or when the volume might not have a free block for every page written behind; a resize writes the file's pages back first.
A channel also keeps fids (node(5)'s `openFid`): files and directories open on it without channels of their own, with its rights, until they are clunked or it closes.
A file's channel or fid keeps the file while it is open: removed, it is gone from its directory, and is freed when its last one closes.
`memory` gives a copy of a file's bytes, which can't run.
It speaks the fs protocol too, which fdio's truncation, rename and sync use: typed attributes, listings with them, renames between directories, a file's resize, and sync.
In it a directory also takes:

- `query`: the files whose attributes match a query (query(6)), through a declared index (`needsIndex` without one, unless the call allows a scan), as their nodes and paths.
- `live`: a channel on which a query's matches come now, then `current`, then each file as it starts or stops matching or changes; `reset` and the matches again if the volume's journal has moved past the query.
- `declareIndex` and `indices`: an index on an attribute, filled from what is there before the call returns.
- `wholeListing`: the directory's whole listing in one read-only memory object, once a batched listing has been served from start to end and the volume hasn't changed since (`cold` otherwise); the next batched listings come from what it kept.

A file takes `map`: a read-only child of the file's VMO, its bytes at their own offsets, kept current as the file is written or resized, so a read is a copy from memory; with it the volume's slot table, read-only, and the file's slot there, which says the file's size under a seqlock (Mapped.swift).
A rename anywhere or the file's removal marks the slot gone, and its reader looks the file up again, as it does for bytes past what it mapped; a removed file's VMO stays while a child of it is held.
A file also takes `capability(writable:)`: a confined channel to that file alone, read-only when `writable` is false, which walks nowhere but to itself (a walk of no names) and cannot remove or rename the file, as tracker(4)'s file chooser returns to a calling window.

Every change is in memory at once.
A transaction group commits a second after the first change since the last, sooner when many blocks or pages written behind wait, and at once on a directory's sync; a file's sync writes the intent log instead.
Either writes back every file's pages first.
Watchers and live queries hear of a change as it is made in memory, so they may hear of one a crash then loses.

# EXAMPLES

Copy a build from the dev machine in a cpu session:

```rc
cp /mnt/term/root/tmp/prog /data/prog
```

Take a snapshot, see a file as it was, and delete the snapshot:

```rc
echo snap >/svc/fs/ctl
cat /svc/fs/status
cat /svc/fs/snap/2026/1030/prog >/data/prog.old
echo unsnap 2026/1030 >/svc/fs/ctl
```

Unlock an encrypted volume at boot, then add a second passphrase and remove the first:

```rc
echo unlock correct horse battery staple >/svc/fs/ctl
echo slot add passphrase a second one >/svc/fs/ctl
echo slot remove 0 >/svc/fs/ctl
```

Let the swarm member node1, at 192.168.0.31, unlock this node's volume at boot, with the key node1's swarm status gives (swarm(4)):

```rc
echo slot add swarm tcp!192.168.0.31!17021 3b6a27bc… >/svc/fs/ctl
cat /svc/fs/status
```

